# Lua in documents

Documents run [Lua 5.4](https://www.lua.org/manual/5.4/) as they are parsed. A script computes text, builds elements, reacts to the document's elements, or defines new syntax.

## Blocks and roles {#blocks}

`:lua` and a fenced block runs code. ``:lua`…` `` in a line shows the value of an expression, or runs statements and shows nothing.

:example
````
:lua
```
function greet(name)
  return "Hello, " .. name
end
```

:lua`greet("you")`, and two plus two
is :lua`2 + 2`.:lua`x = 1`
````

What a role shows depends on the value:

| Value | Shows |
|:------|:------|
| A string or a number | Text. It is never markup: `"**"` shows two stars. |
| An element, from [[elements#|nml.element]] | That element. |
| A list | Each of its values, in order. |
| `nil` | Nothing. |

To write markup, a script parses it with `nml.parse`: see [[elements#parse]].

## The state of a document {#state}

Each document has one Lua state: what a script defines, the scripts after it see, in the document and in the files it [[documents#include|includes]]. Documents do not share state; [[documents#import|modules]] share code.

A script runs where it is, as the document is parsed: it sees what comes before it, not after. [[deferred#]] shows how to compute content from the whole document, or from the whole workspace.

## The sandbox {#sandbox}

Scripts get Lua's base functions, and the `string`, `table`, `math`, `utf8` and `coroutine` libraries. There is no `io`, `os`, `require` or `load`: what a script reads from outside goes through the `nml` table, which records it, so that the page is built again when it changes.

| Function | |
|:---------|:-|
| `nml.read(path)` | A file of the workspace, as text. |
| `nml.env(name)` | An environment variable of the build. |
| `nml.time()` | When the build started, in seconds since 1970. `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` sets it, for reproducible builds. |
| `nml.date(time)` | The calendar fields of a time, in UTC. |
| `nml.variable(name)` | The value of a [[variables#|variable]], as written. |

:example
```
Built in :lua`nml.date().year`.
```

A document's scripts may run 100 million Lua instructions and use 64 MiB together: a script that loops forever is an error, not a hung build.

## Errors {#errors}

A Lua error is an error of the document, where the script is, with Lua's message and the line in the script. Editors show it as you type.

## In editors

The language server completes and documents Lua when [LuaLS](https://github.com/LuaLS/lua-language-server) is installed: it sees a document's scripts together, with the `nml` API declared (see [[api#]]). `nml init` writes the declarations to `.nml/types/`.
